module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraDescentOpenSubspace
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.QuotientRingSheafPushforward

/-!
# The quotient of the structure sheaf of an open subspace, and the slice quotient

Let `X` be a ringed space and `U` an open of it. A quotient of the structure sheaf over `U` can
be formed in two places, and the local-to-global step of [Stacks, Tag 0BQY] meets both:

* on the **slice site** `(Opens X).over U`, where the gluing argument runs, as a quotient `R'` of
  `O_X|_U`, read on the open subspace by the pushforward
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingSheaf` of
  `StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraDescentOpenSubspace`;
* on the **restricted ringed space** `X.restrict U`, where every local statement of the Koszul
  development lives, as `Stacks.Sites.quotientRingSheaf` of a sheaf of ideals of
  `O_{X|_U}` — for the Koszul statements, the ideal generated by the restrictions of the
  generating sections.

`GradedAlgebraDescentOpenSubspace` takes the quotient downstairs to *be* the pushforward of the
quotient upstairs, which is what makes the descent square there degenerate, and says in so many
words that a consumer holding a quotient formed independently on `X.restrict U` still owes the
identification of the two. **This file supplies that identification.**

It is not bookkeeping: a quotient is a sheafification, and the pushforward of a sheafification is
not a sheafification of anything, so the two sheaves are not the same construction read twice.
What identifies them is that both are locally surjective quotient maps out of the structure sheaf
of `X.restrict U` with the same kernel, and that a quotient is determined by its kernel —
`Stacks.Sites.quotientRingSheafIso`, by way of its pushforward form
`Stacks.Sites.pushforwardQuotientRingIso`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceQuotientRingIso`: **the identification**, for sheaves of
  commutative rings. The quotient of the structure sheaf of `X.restrict U` by a sheaf of ideals
  whose sections over an open `W ⊆ U` are the sections of a sheaf of ideals `I` of `O_X|_U` over
  the corresponding object of `Opens X / U` is the pushforward of `(O_X|_U)/I`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingQuotientIso`: the same identification read in sheaves of
  (not necessarily commutative) rings, where its target is literally
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingSheaf` — the form a consumer of the descent transport
  needs.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.quotientRingHom_comp_openSubspaceRingQuotientIso_hom`: the
  identification carries the quotient map formed on `X.restrict U` to
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingHom` of the slice quotient map, so it identifies the
  quotient *maps* and not merely the two sheaves of rings.

## Implementation notes

**The hypothesis on the two ideals is the content.** Different sheaves of ideals have different
quotients, so there is no identification to be had without knowing that the ideal used downstairs
agrees with the one used upstairs; and since the two are submodules of sheaves on different
sites, the only form in which the agreement can be stated is on sections. The canonical ideal
satisfying it is `Stacks.Sites.pushforwardKerIdeal`, whose sections are computed by
`Stacks.Sites.sectionIdeal_pushforwardKerIdeal`, so the hypothesis is satisfiable.

**What this does and does not discharge of `hBV`.** The hypothesis `hBV` of
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.isIso_desc_app_of_coversTop` asks that the
restriction of the descended composition-product algebra to the slice site at a member of the
cover be an exterior algebra on the restricted generator. The route to it runs through
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.descendOpenSubspaceHom`, whose quotient downstairs is the pushforward;
this file identifies that quotient with the one the local Koszul statements use, which is the
step `GradedAlgebraDescentOpenSubspace` recorded as owed. The transport of
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.descend` itself along such an identification of the quotient —
`descend` is taken along a *named* morphism of sheaves of rings, so an isomorphism of its targets
does not by itself move the descended algebra — is no longer owed either:
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.descendRingIsoHom` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraDescentQuotientIso` is that transport,
and `isExteriorAlgebra_descend_of_ringIso` carries the universal property of an exterior algebra
across it. Its instantiation at the spelling below is proved too, in
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.QuotientRingSheafOpenSubspaceIdeal`, as
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_descend_openSubspaceRingQuotient`; what it has to clear
is that the source of `Stacks.Sites.quotientRingHom` on the slice is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (X.sheaf.over U)` while
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingHom` reads its argument out of
`(structureSheafRing X).over U`, one
sheaf of rings under two spellings that elaboration crosses and instance search does not, and the
resolution is to route through one spelling rather than to thread instance hypotheses. The
hypothesis `hI'` below is supplied there for the sheaves of ideals a morphism generates, which is
the case the Koszul statements are in: those are local closures, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionIdeal_idealOfHom_openSubspace` reduces `hI'` for them to an
objectwise agreement of the two images, local closure crossing the site equivalence by
`Stacks.Sites.functorPullback_mem_iff`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BQY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BQY)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0942](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0942)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Sheaf Opposite TopologicalSpace
open AlgebraicGeometry RingedSpaceModuleAdjunction

universe u

namespace Stacks.SheafOfModules

open Stacks.Sites

variable {X : RingedSpace.{u, u}} (U : Opens X.carrier)
  [HasWeakSheafify (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U) CommRingCat.{u}]
  [HasWeakSheafify ((Opens.grothendieckTopology X.carrier).over U) CommRingCat.{u}]
  (I : Stacks.Sites.IdealSubsheaf.{u, u, u} (X.sheaf.over U))

/-- **The identification of the two quotients of the structure sheaf over `U`.** Let `I` be a
sheaf of ideals of the slice structure sheaf `O_X|_U` and let `I'` be a sheaf of ideals of the
structure sheaf of `X.restrict U` whose sections over an open `W ⊆ U` are the sections of `I`
over the corresponding object of `Opens X / U`. Then the quotient of the structure sheaf of
`X.restrict U` by `I'`, formed there, is the pushforward of `(O_X|_U)/I` off the slice site —
compatibly with the two quotient maps, by
`Stacks.Sites.toQuotientRingSheaf_comp_quotientRingSheafIso_hom`.

This is `Stacks.Sites.pushforwardQuotientRingIso` at the site functor
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceSiteFunctor`, which is continuous and cocontinuous, being a
dense subsite in both directions. -/
def openSubspaceQuotientRingIso (I' : Stacks.Sites.IdealSubsheaf.{u, u, u} (openSubspace U).sheaf)
    (hI' : ∀ V : (Opens ↥U)ᵒᵖ, sectionIdeal I' V =
      sectionIdeal I (op ((openSubspaceSiteFunctor U).obj V.unop))) :
    quotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} I' ≅
      (commRingPushforward (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U)
        (openSubspaceSiteFunctor U)).obj (quotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} I) :=
  pushforwardQuotientRingIso (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U) (openSubspaceSiteFunctor U) I I' hI'

/-- **The identification of the two quotients, read in sheaves of rings.** The target is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingSheaf` at the quotient `(O_X|_U)/I` of the slice
structure sheaf: the open-subspace reading of that quotient. The source is the quotient formed on
`X.restrict U` itself. This is the form in which the local-to-global step of [Stacks, Tag 0BQY]
needs the comparison, the descent of a sheaf of graded algebras being taken over a sheaf of
(not necessarily commutative) rings. -/
def openSubspaceRingQuotientIso (I' : Stacks.Sites.IdealSubsheaf.{u, u, u} (openSubspace U).sheaf)
    (hI' : ∀ V : (Opens ↥U)ᵒᵖ, sectionIdeal I' V =
      sectionIdeal I (op ((openSubspaceSiteFunctor U).obj V.unop))) :
    toRingCat (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U) (quotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} I') ≅
      openSubspaceRingSheaf U
        (R' := toRingCat ((Opens.grothendieckTopology X.carrier).over U)
          (quotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} I)) :=
  (sheafCompose (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U)
      (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})).mapIso (openSubspaceQuotientRingIso U I I' hI')

/-- **The identification carries the quotient map formed on `X.restrict U` to the open-subspace
reading of the slice quotient map**, so it identifies the quotient *maps* and not merely the two
sheaves of rings. This is the form in which a consumer comparing two descents needs it: the
descent of a sheaf of graded algebras is taken along a named morphism of sheaves of rings, and
these are the two named morphisms. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem quotientRingHom_comp_openSubspaceRingQuotientIso_hom
    (I' : Stacks.Sites.IdealSubsheaf.{u, u, u} (openSubspace U).sheaf)
    (hI' : ∀ V : (Opens ↥U)ᵒᵖ, sectionIdeal I' V =
      sectionIdeal I (op ((openSubspaceSiteFunctor U).obj V.unop))) :
    quotientRingHom I' ≫ (openSubspaceRingQuotientIso U I I' hI').hom =
      openSubspaceRingHom U (quotientRingHom I) :=
  congrArg
    (fun g => (sheafCompose (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U)
      (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})).map g)
    (toQuotientRingSheaf_comp_quotientRingSheafIso_hom
      (pushforwardToQuotientRingSheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U)
        (openSubspaceSiteFunctor U) I) I' fun V =>
        (ker_pushforwardToQuotientRingSheaf_app (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥U)
          (openSubspaceSiteFunctor U) I V).trans (hI' V).symm)

end Stacks.SheafOfModules

end
